WEBVTT

00:00:00.262 --> 00:00:02.079
Here's a spanner on a stuck nut.

00:00:02.603 --> 00:00:04.490
Its handle is twenty-five centimetres long,

00:00:05.094 --> 00:00:07.121
and each push on the end is eighty

00:00:07.202 --> 00:00:07.603
newtons.

00:00:08.486 --> 00:00:10.447
One push is at right angles to the

00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:10.767
handle,

00:00:11.409 --> 00:00:12.849
one is at thirty degrees to it,

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.971
and one points straight along the handle, towards

00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:16.352
the nut.

00:00:17.056 --> 00:00:18.442
Same force, same spot.

00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:19.863
Rank them.

00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:21.731
Which turns the nut best,

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:23.998
and does any of them do nothing at

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:24.199
all?

00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:30.693
Here's how strongly each push tries to turn

00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:32.697
the nut, shown by a curved arrow.

00:00:33.362 --> 00:00:35.755
The right-angle push has the biggest turning effect.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.853
The thirty degree push has one only half

00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:39.255
as big.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.264
And the push along the handle just shoves

00:00:42.345 --> 00:00:44.108
on the nut, with no turning effect at

00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:44.308
all.

00:00:45.331 --> 00:00:47.115
The turning effect of a force about a

00:00:47.196 --> 00:00:48.780
pivot is called torque.

00:00:49.505 --> 00:00:50.496
Torque isn't a force.

00:00:50.962 --> 00:00:53.030
It's what a force does to rotation.

00:00:53.814 --> 00:00:56.103
So why does the same force give more

00:00:56.184 --> 00:00:57.791
torque at some angles than others?

00:00:58.803 --> 00:01:00.894
Start with the simplest case, the push at

00:01:01.035 --> 00:01:01.578
right angles.

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:04.565
A longer spanner makes a stuck nut easier,

00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:06.950
and a door pushed near its hinge barely

00:01:06.991 --> 00:01:07.310
moves.

00:01:07.994 --> 00:01:09.600
Distance from the pivot matters.

00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:12.227
Push halfway along the handle, and you get

00:01:12.308 --> 00:01:13.711
only half the turning effect.

00:01:14.614 --> 00:01:16.277
For a force at right angles to the

00:01:16.318 --> 00:01:18.441
line from the pivot, the torque is the

00:01:18.542 --> 00:01:19.944
distance times the force.

00:01:20.588 --> 00:01:22.001
Tau equals r F.

00:01:22.828 --> 00:01:25.793
Here that's zero point two five metres times

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:26.634
eighty newtons,

00:01:27.216 --> 00:01:28.437
twenty newton metres.

00:01:29.241 --> 00:01:31.055
Torque is measured in newton metres.

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:33.465
It isn't energy, so we never write it

00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:34.087
in joules.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:36.722
Now the push at thirty degrees.

00:01:37.368 --> 00:01:38.842
Split it into two components.

00:01:39.388 --> 00:01:40.783
One runs along the handle.

00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:42.898
It just pulls on the nut through its

00:01:42.919 --> 00:01:44.246
centre, so it can't turn it.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.899
The other is at right angles to the

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.310
handle, and only that part does the turning.

00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:53.100
It's F sin theta, where theta is the

00:01:53.281 --> 00:01:55.004
angle between the handle and the force.

00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:58.291
Eighty times sin thirty is forty newtons,

00:01:58.853 --> 00:02:01.056
so the torque is zero point two five

00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:01.777
times forty,

00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:03.380
ten newton metres.

00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:06.190
Half the force at right angles, so half

00:02:06.211 --> 00:02:06.572
the torque.

00:02:07.318 --> 00:02:08.489
There's a second way to see it.

00:02:09.076 --> 00:02:11.467
Extend the force arrow into a long line,

00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:12.874
called its line of action.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.531
Now find the shortest distance from the pivot

00:02:15.653 --> 00:02:16.276
to that line.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.888
It meets the line at right angles, and

00:02:18.929 --> 00:02:21.519
it's called the perpendicular distance, or the moment

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:21.740
arm.

00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:24.910
Here it's zero point two five times sin

00:02:24.951 --> 00:02:26.634
thirty, twelve and a half centimetres.

00:02:27.318 --> 00:02:29.727
Eighty newtons times zero point one two five

00:02:29.788 --> 00:02:31.835
metres is ten newton metres again.

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:35.885
Either way, tau equals r F sin theta,

00:02:36.307 --> 00:02:38.710
which you'll also see written as r perpendicular

00:02:39.052 --> 00:02:39.492
times F.

00:02:40.322 --> 00:02:40.563
So

00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:43.240
what about the push straight along the handle?

00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:45.947
Its line of action passes right through the

00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:48.612
pivot, so the moment arm is zero, and

00:02:48.672 --> 00:02:49.414
so is the torque.

00:02:50.115 --> 00:02:52.840
In the formula, theta is the angle between

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:53.541
two arrows:

00:02:54.263 --> 00:02:55.785
one from the pivot out to where the

00:02:55.825 --> 00:02:56.466
force acts,

00:02:57.007 --> 00:02:57.989
and the force itself.

00:02:58.732 --> 00:03:01.502
Pushing towards the nut, those arrows point opposite

00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:03.650
ways, so theta is one hundred and eighty

00:03:03.691 --> 00:03:04.092
degrees.

00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:06.712
Pulling outwards, theta is zero.

00:03:07.338 --> 00:03:08.732
The sine of either is zero.

00:03:09.298 --> 00:03:11.046
No turning, however hard you push.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.173
That's why a door won't open if you

00:03:13.214 --> 00:03:15.159
push its edge straight towards the hinges.

00:03:15.701 --> 00:03:16.968
Put it together on a graph.

00:03:17.551 --> 00:03:19.261
Torque follows sin theta.

00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:22.625
It's zero along the handle, grows as the

00:03:22.665 --> 00:03:25.827
force swings round, peaks at ninety degrees, and

00:03:25.907 --> 00:03:27.668
falls back to zero at one eighty.

00:03:28.372 --> 00:03:29.686
That's the answer to our question.

00:03:30.331 --> 00:03:31.914
Only the part of the force at right

00:03:31.994 --> 00:03:33.377
angles to the handle turns it,

00:03:33.918 --> 00:03:35.721
and that part is biggest when the whole

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:37.124
force is at right angles.

00:03:37.869 --> 00:03:39.161
Torque also has a direction.

00:03:39.808 --> 00:03:41.737
This push turns the nut anticlockwise.

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:43.006
Push the opposite way,

00:03:43.511 --> 00:03:44.498
and it turns it clockwise.

00:03:45.138 --> 00:03:46.250
Two slips to avoid.

00:03:46.896 --> 00:03:49.124
First, using the full handle length with an

00:03:49.345 --> 00:03:51.894
angled force, as if tau were always r

00:03:51.915 --> 00:03:51.954
F.

00:03:53.682 --> 00:03:56.094
For the thirty degree push, that gives twenty

00:03:56.376 --> 00:03:56.999
instead of ten.

00:03:57.926 --> 00:03:59.762
Second, using the wrong angle.

00:04:00.444 --> 00:04:02.165
If a question gives the angle between the

00:04:02.246 --> 00:04:04.506
force and a line at right angles to

00:04:04.547 --> 00:04:06.928
the handle, the sine of that angle is

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:07.869
the wrong factor.

00:04:08.751 --> 00:04:10.760
Always use the angle between the force and

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.790
the line from the pivot to where it

00:04:12.932 --> 00:04:13.132
acts.

00:04:13.756 --> 00:04:14.783
Here's the short version.

00:04:15.348 --> 00:04:17.124
Push square, push far.

00:04:17.688 --> 00:04:20.050
Square means at right angles to the line

00:04:20.071 --> 00:04:20.610
from the pivot,

00:04:21.132 --> 00:04:23.434
and far means a long moment arm.

00:04:24.036 --> 00:04:25.666
Both make the turning effect bigger.

00:04:26.429 --> 00:04:28.330
And if the force's line of action passes

00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:28.971
through the pivot,

00:04:29.652 --> 00:04:30.653
no amount of pushing helps.

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:33.707
Your turn.

00:04:34.736 --> 00:04:37.037
A force of forty newtons acts on the

00:04:37.258 --> 00:04:38.098
end of a lever,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.462
zero point three zero metres from its pivot.

00:04:42.365 --> 00:04:44.549
Calculate the torque about the pivot when the

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.374
force is at right angles to the lever,

00:04:46.996 --> 00:04:48.799
and when it's at thirty degrees to the

00:04:48.841 --> 00:04:49.060
lever.

00:04:49.865 --> 00:04:50.690
Pause here to answer.

00:05:01.294 --> 00:05:03.575
At right angles, tau equals r F,

00:05:04.117 --> 00:05:07.218
zero point three zero times forty, twelve newton

00:05:07.239 --> 00:05:07.539
metres.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.182
At thirty degrees, multiply by sin thirty, a

00:05:11.223 --> 00:05:13.804
half, giving six point zero newton metres.

00:05:14.566 --> 00:05:17.300
The classic slip is using cos thirty, which

00:05:17.341 --> 00:05:18.606
gives about ten point four.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.122
The angle is between the force and the

00:05:21.224 --> 00:05:22.451
lever, so it's sine.

00:05:24.044 --> 00:05:24.492
Last one.

00:05:25.702 --> 00:05:28.164
A force of forty newtons acts on the

00:05:28.344 --> 00:05:30.906
end of a zero point three zero metre

00:05:30.946 --> 00:05:34.009
handle, directed along the handle towards the pivot.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.312
State the moment arm and the torque about

00:05:37.352 --> 00:05:37.713
the pivot,

00:05:38.373 --> 00:05:40.215
and the angle between the force and the

00:05:40.275 --> 00:05:42.137
line from the pivot to the point where

00:05:42.157 --> 00:05:42.577
it acts.

00:05:43.402 --> 00:05:44.293
Pause here to answer.

00:05:55.148 --> 00:05:57.331
The line of action passes through the pivot,

00:05:57.753 --> 00:06:00.116
so the moment arm is zero, and the

00:06:00.177 --> 00:06:02.220
torque is zero newton metres.

00:06:03.222 --> 00:06:05.223
The line from the pivot points out along

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.525
the handle, and the force points back in,

00:06:07.946 --> 00:06:09.906
so the angle is one hundred and eighty

00:06:09.947 --> 00:06:10.387
degrees.

00:06:11.309 --> 00:06:13.396
Sin one eighty is zero, which agrees.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.161
The slip is writing r F,

00:06:16.782 --> 00:06:19.483
twelve newton metres, because the force is far

00:06:19.564 --> 00:06:20.164
from the pivot.

00:06:20.848 --> 00:06:22.883
Distance alone doesn't turn anything.

00:06:24.575 --> 00:06:27.661
So one force's torque depends on its size,

00:06:27.963 --> 00:06:30.328
its distance from the pivot, and its angle.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.732
But real objects usually have several forces turning

00:06:33.753 --> 00:06:36.954
them at once, some clockwise and some anticlockwise.

00:06:37.836 --> 00:06:39.958
A tower crane holds a heavy load far

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:42.440
out on one side, and a counterweight close

00:06:42.481 --> 00:06:43.101
in on the other.

00:06:43.824 --> 00:06:45.491
How do those turning effects balance?

00:06:48.161 --> 00:06:48.984
Here are the key notes.

00:06:49.507 --> 00:06:51.055
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:51.095 --> 00:06:51.517
to copy them.
